thank you all for joining us here I'm  happy to share with you some mischief  that I've been getting into the last six  months I became Dean here about a year  and a half ago and was curious about why  the outcomes in our graduate programs  varied so much in terms of what I was  thinking about in terms of outcomes  included the fraction of students who  finished each degree program the  fraction of students who finished within  six years and then we were also curious  about why students dropped out of some  programs after their third year without  getting a PhD so we measured those  outcome parameters and then I was  curious about why if this might be true  because the students are basically the  same you can't tell any difference among  the students in the various programs  they have the same kind of  qualifications and so on they're all  bright apparently motivated students so  we went looking for a an explanation and  to the survey of the departments and  programs and ask them how they manage  their graduate programs what kind of  support they provided to their students  not financial support because that's the  same across the board but what kind of  academic support how often that they met  with their students and in and what kind  of interactions they had and the  remarkable result of this analysis was  that there is a very strong correlation  between the strength of the academic  support the strength of the mentoring  and the outcomes we have a report that  we published about this in August it's  called improving bridge and education at  Yale and the graphs are way too small  for you to see from the back of the room  but I'll pretend I'm the graph here this  axis i'm going to write the the  intensity of the support by the faculty  on a dis axis the outcomes and the data  are scattered like this but there's a  very strong correlation between good  practices and good outcomes and they  actually sort of fall into two of the  four quadrants a group down in the lower  left-hand quadrant where the  cops aren't so good and support isn't so  good and another group that's up in the  upper right-hand quadrant where the  support is good and the outcomes are  good now the this correlation doesn't  explain all the variables in the system  there are extenuating circumstances some  fields it just takes a little bit longer  and so on but this is actually very good  news because what this tells us is if we  could identify what the good practices  are in managing a graduate program and  if we shared them with everybody and if  I could convince the faculty they  implement all these good mentoring  practices I am confident that the  outcomes would approve so we came to  this conclusion last summer and started  to talk with some of the programs that  we started with the ones that were in  the lower left-hand quadrant that were  struggling and it turned out by simply  sitting down with a chair and the  director of graduate studies in most of  these programs that we actually  accomplished a lot of our goals because  simply by laying out where they stood  and we identified where they were on the  graph to them but not to anybody else  and going through the things that they  might or might not be doing we  identified the sort of a to-do list for  them to improve their programs and by  now we've seen about Oh 45 or 50 of the  graduate programs and virtually everyone  is appreciative of our effort to  identify how they could do better we  also collected a beginning collection of  best practices this is 30 pages of best  practices from across the university and  we found all sorts of fascinating  creative techniques that people were  using to help their graduate students  and of course we shared this and and our  report with all the graduate students  and all the faculty across the  university so everybody has access to  these good ideas our best apartments in  fact our highest performing departments  didn't need any encouragement such as  Peters 