hey can  finish their book and make a bigger mark  in their field we need to help our  students with placement  the faculty tell me that they simply  don't have the expertise to help their  students with placement outside of  academic community personally I find  that rather amazing since a one more  than half of biologists go into  something other than becoming a  professor so we're all used to do that  those sort of outcomes but in the  humanities they say they know how to  help them get into the museum business  or our other lines of work so we're  going to have to help them with that but  this is where the alumni can be helpful  to us one of the things we need to do is  to expand our students horizons so they  think outside of the academic community  into all the wonderful things you can do  with a the training to get in a PhD  program and we've been working with  graduate school alumni association they  see some of my friends here in the front  trying to pops with my ideas about how  to do that we were talking about  internships the idea of this week or  also you know is something simpler than  an internship maybe just an opportunity  to shadow somebody who's running a  museum or are working in private  industry or something like that spend a  week with somebody just to see what the  heck it's all about and we think that  just as a relatively simple exposure to  opportunities outside of the academic  community will help them think about  their futures so that's where that's the  mischief I've been getting into the last  16 months and I'm thrilled that we have  a dozens of serious discussions going on  around campus about how to improve  graduate education thank  i should point out if you're interested  to look at these documents they are  available in the graduate school website
 

